    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. When you mention these \"unseen fuel reservoirs\" or \"cumulative issues,\" how do we categorize the unpredictability of it all? Sometimes it feels like these failures happen randomly, even when maintenance logs look fine. Is there a way to quantify that sense of sudden, unexpected escalation in a risk assessment?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This unpredictability is often categorized using probabilistic risk assessment methods, which move beyond simple checklists. Techniques like Fault Tree Analysis (FTA) or Event Tree Analysis (ETA) help map out how multiple small, seemingly random failures can converge to cause a major incident. By mathematically determining the likelihood of these initiating events occurring simultaneously, you transition the vague \"suddenness\" into a quantifiable risk rating. This framework allows assessors to focus mitigation efforts on the critical failure pathways, not just isolated equipment checks.",
      "source": "target",
